  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{2x-2}=2\\cdot {9}^{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-2} & =2\\cdot {9}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2x-2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2\\cdot {9}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-2=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2\\cdot {9}^{x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({9}^{x}) \\\\ 2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(9) \\\\ 2x-x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(9) & =2+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) \\\\ x(2-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(9)) & =2+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}{2-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
